    1	"""
    2	Hankel singular-value test for hidden linear-recurrence structure in chi_P.
    3	
    4	Idea (fresh perspective, session 58):
    5	   If the prime indicator chi_P(n) satisfies *any* finite-order linear
    6	   recurrence over the reals (or, by extension, any near-low-rank
    7	   approximate recurrence), then the Hankel matrix
    8	        H[i, j]  =  chi_P(i + j)         (0 <= i, j < K)
    9	   has rank r equal to the order of that recurrence (Berlekamp-Massey).
    10	
    11	   The prime indicator is *not* expected to obey such a recurrence -- but
    12	   that's also what we expect for a random Bernoulli sequence with the
    13	   same density.  The interesting question is whether real primes are
    14	   either:
    15	     (a) *more* compressible than random (lower effective rank, hidden
    16	         recurrence-like structure), or
    17	     (b) *less* compressible than random (anti-correlated, somehow more
    18	         "spread out" than chance), or
    19	     (c) statistically indistinguishable.
    20	
    21	   We compute singular values of H_chi and H_random, look at the decay,
    22	   and report effective ranks at several thresholds.
    23	
    24	   This is a different basis than DCT, wavelet, or Walsh -- it directly
    25	   tests for linear-recurrence structure (hidden algebraic generators).
    26	
    27	Pass / fail
    28	   FAIL  -> SV spectra of H_chi and H_random are statistically
    29	            indistinguishable (effective ranks within ~1 sigma).
    30	            Adds a 23rd pseudorandomness measure.
